<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Bamana Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1712 CE – 1861 CE · capital Segou</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful Bambara kingdom ruled from Segou on the Niger, built by slave soldiers and destroyed by the jihad of Umar Tall in 1861.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1712 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biton-coulibaly">Mamari Kulibali</a>, known as Biton, turned a young men's association of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bambara</a> hunters and farmers into a standing army and made himself king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Segou</a>. He defeated rivals, raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> valley and created a kingdom that dominated the middle Niger from Djenne towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The army, the tonjon, was made up largely of war captives bound to the king, and in 1766 its commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngolo-diarra">Ngolo Diarra</a> seized the throne and founded a new dynasty. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-monzon-diarra">Da Monzon Diarra</a> in the early 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Segou</a> reached its height. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bambara</a> followed traditional religion, while Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marka">Marka</a> and Somono traders and boatmen handled commerce. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mungo-park">Mungo Park</a> passed through Segou in 1796.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Segou</a> fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massina-empire">Massina Empire</a> for decades but fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-hadj-umar-tall">Umar Tall</a>, who took the city in March 1861 and made it the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/toucouleur-empire">Toucouleur Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bambara</a> memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biton-coulibaly">Biton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-monzon-diarra">Da Monzon</a> lives on in epic poetry.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bamana-empire.jpg" alt="Bamana Empire" width="320" height="159"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Eynaud · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Africa_de_l%27O%C3%A8st_(fin_s%C3%A8gle_XVIII).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1712 CE – 1861 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Segou</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Biton Kulibali, Ngolo Diarra, Da Monzon Diarra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Bambara traditional religion, Sunni Islam among traders</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bambara</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Segou Empire, Kingdom of Segou, Bambara Empire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1786&amp;focus=bamana-empire" aria-label="Bamana Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4510 1677 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bamana Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4830 1933L4830 1933"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4830 1933h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4830 1933h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4830 1933h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Segou. <a href="/atlas?year=1786&amp;focus=bamana-empire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
